      "pageTitle": "Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "Reporters were threatened with physical removal before the briefing, and Hegseth largely ignored questions from the traditional Pentagon press corps",
      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt Reporters were threatened with physical removal before the briefing, and Hegseth largely ignored questions from the traditional Pentagon press corps Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION What happened at Pete Hegseth&rsquo;s first press conference in months Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth speaks during a press conference at the Pentagon in Washington on Tuesday. (AP Photo/Nathan Howard) For a guy facing plenty of heat for his role in leading the U.S. war effort in Iran, Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth isn&rsquo;t exactly trying to get the media on his side. Before a press conference at the Pentagon on Tuesday &mdash; the first in six months &mdash; a security agent for Hegseth warned reporters that they would be physically removed if they didn&rsquo;t act professionally. Let me repeat that: physically removed . The Washington Post&rsquo;s Tara Copp, who attended the press conference, wrote , &ldquo;The unusual warning was a first in recent memory, members of the Pentagon press corps said. Reporters, including from The Washington Post, requested clarification and whether that meant shouting questions would result in removal, but the security agent provided no answer and a Defense Department civilian staffer did not elaborate.&rdquo; Copp added, &ldquo;Joel Valdez, one of Hegseth&rsquo;s political staffers, later offered to explain the rules but said that he would do so only off the record. When several reporters objected, Valdez walked off.&rdquo; Hegseth&rsquo;s press conference lasted about 30 minutes and &mdash; good news &mdash; no one was removed, physically or otherwise. But during the question-and-answer portion, Hegseth called on reporters from outlets friendly to him and the administration. Copp wrote on X , &ldquo;I shouted at least 4 questions that Hegseth did not answer, on whether he takes responsibility for the war; on war crimes; others shouted if he&rsquo;d become a distraction for Trump. Hegseth only took Qs from Conservative media or new media outlets they brought in after kicking out the Pentagon press corps last year. Almost everyone who got questions, save for our colleague Mike Glenn, had agreed to the strict rules that the Pentagon Press Corps refused to sign last year.&rdquo; Hegseth met with the press a day after Republican Rep. Thomas Massie introduced eight articles of impeachment against him. (That impeachment bid failed, as I&rsquo;ll get to in a minute.) Hegseth said, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s a farce and it&rsquo;s a stunt and everybody knows it.&rdquo; Hegseth also said Massie&rsquo;s actions put &ldquo;a target on the backs of American warriors by signaling that Congress will undermine their missions in real time.&rdquo; Hegseth added, &ldquo;This is not patriotism. This is sabotage. &hellip; Put the secretary on trial today, and the men and women who carried out those orders tomorrow.&rdquo; Before House Republicans banded together to block the impeachment, Hegseth predicted that the effort would fail, just as previous impeachment efforts against President Donald Trump had: &ldquo;They will fail again, and if standing with President Trump puts me in that company, I&rsquo;ll take that company every day of the week.&rdquo; Responding to criticism of the war in Iran, Hegseth said everything he has done &ldquo;was a lawful operation ordered by the commander in chief, then ordered by myself, notified to Congress and executed by American warriors who did their jobs.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;These are not high crimes. This is the job.&rdquo; Trump, who has vowed to stand by Hegseth, graded his defense secretary&rsquo;s press conference performance. Trump wrote on Truth Social , &ldquo;Pete Hegseth gets an &lsquo;A&rsquo; PLUS, PLUS, PLUS for his defense against a disgruntled, angry, defeated, former Congressman, Thomas Massie, a TOTAL LOSER!&rdquo; For more on the press conference, check out The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s Lara Seligman and Michael R. Gordon with &ldquo;Five Takeaways From Hegseth&rsquo;s First Pentagon Press Conference in Months.&rdquo; Hegseth survives As I mentioned, House Republicans blocked an effort to impeach Hegseth, voting to send the articles to committee rather than allowing an impeachment vote. The Washington Post&rsquo;s Tara Copp, Dan Lamothe and Noah Robertson wrote , &ldquo;By a 211-204 vote, lawmakers referred the impeachment articles to a committee for further study, effectively sidelining the measures. Had the effort advanced, lawmakers would have held final votes Wednesday. The result follows a coordinated effort within the Trump administration &mdash; including by Trump himself &mdash; to defend Hegseth.&rdquo; In addition to Republicans sticking with Hegseth, 11 Democrats did not vote. Hegseth&rsquo;s reprieve might be only temporary. The Post wrote, &ldquo;While Hegseth survived Tuesday&rsquo;s vote, he emerged facing more outspoken GOP criticism of his tenure than ever before during his two years as defense secretary. Multiple Republicans who opposed the impeachment still said they were unhappy with Hegseth, and some GOP senators indicated they would have been open to holding a trial had the impeachment advanced. Democrats, who beginning in January will hold sizable majorities in both chambers of Congress, have said they will seek to use their newfound power to remove Hegseth from office.&rdquo; In a Truth Social post , Trump wrote, &ldquo;The Democrats&rsquo; attempt to impeach Secretary of War Pete Hegseth has miserably FAILED! Every single Republican, except Thomas Massie, a major LOSER, voted in favor of Pete. Again, Victories in Venezuela and Iran, the Best Recruitment Numbers in the History of the Military (Long waiting lists to get in!), and Spirit like rarely seen before, led to the EXONERATION of a Great American Patriot. The Democrats continue to weaponize and abuse the United States of America. They must be stopped! MAKE AMERICA GREAT AGAIN.&rdquo; A pressing matter OK, so UFC boss Dana White is not going to be the next White House press secretary. So who will be the next press secretary? There&rsquo;s a school of thought that President Donald Trump might just go without one. After all, during his first administration, Stephanie Grisham was press secretary for nearly a year and didn&rsquo;t hold a single press briefing. Besides, whenever there&rsquo;s a need for a press conference, Trump can just do it himself. However, the White House press secretary has more responsibilities than holding press briefings. And there are reports that Trump continues to look to fill the opening left by Katie Zacharia, who replaced Karoline Leavitt but was pushed out after five days and one dumpster fire of a press conference. Fox News Digital&rsquo;s Peter D'Abrosca and Katelyn Caralle reported that sources familiar with the search say Trump is leaning toward picking a man to fill the spot, and that a leading candidate may be conservative commentator Ben Ferguson, who hosts a nationally syndicated radio show and is a political commentator on CNN. One unnamed source told Fox News, &ldquo;POTUS is not sold on the idea. I think it&rsquo;s safe to say (Ferguson is) pursuing the job and the president is aware.&rdquo; Another name that remains in the mix is CNN&rsquo;s Scott Jennings. A source told Fox News, &ldquo;The president keeps going back and forth on Scott Jennings. &hellip; I don&rsquo;t think it will happen.&rdquo; Fox News Digital added, &ldquo;A few other names have floated around but not emerged as clear picks for the job &mdash; this includes White House Communications Director Steven Cheung, Breitbart chief Matthew Boyle and Ari Fleischer, who was White House press secretary during President George W. Bush&rsquo;s first term. Many women have also made the short list, but the two sources who spoke with Fox News Digital insist the next person to hold the job will likely be a man.&rdquo; Here&rsquo;s the truth TheRighting reports that Donald Trump&rsquo;s Truth Social attracted only 11.8 million visits in October &mdash; a significant drop from the 19.5 million visits in October 2025. In the report, Howard Polskin, president of TheRighting, said, &ldquo;The steep drop in visits to Trump&rsquo;s Truth Social coincides with a shift in the American public&rsquo;s perception on the effectiveness and consequences of Trump&rsquo;s war with Iran. The traffic to Truth Social tells the story. In April 2026, when large swaths of the public were still behind Trump&rsquo;s war, Truth Social attracted 48.8 million visits, the most in the past 15 months. But in May, year-over-year visits began sliding resulting in a six-month skid that continues to this day.&rdquo; Polskin added, &ldquo;Looking back at the last six months, it&rsquo;s clear that Truth Social&rsquo;s plunging traffic was an early warning foretelling the GOP&rsquo;s disastrous midterm election results that were yoked to Trump. The historically low October 2026 Truth Social traffic, which is the president&rsquo;s preferred megaphone for spewing his lies and insults, can be interpreted as a referendum on the president&rsquo;s popularity. Trump is literally being tuned out. It will be fascinating to see if the decline continues in November.&rdquo; Trump&rsquo;s kind words In this 2025 photo, President-elect Donald Trump and Melania Trump are greeted by President Joe Biden and first lady Jill Biden at the White House. (AP Photo/Alex Brandon) In Tuesday&rsquo;s newsletter , I wrote about how former President Joe Biden has a new memoir &mdash; &ldquo;Promise Me, America&rdquo; &mdash; coming out next week. The Atlantic published an excerpt from the book: &ldquo;What Trump Did on January 6 &lsquo;Should Follow Him to His Grave.&rsquo;&rdquo; In that excerpt, Biden recalled how Trump came to the White House eight days after defeating Kamala Harris in the 2024 election and lavished him with praise, telling him, &ldquo;You know, Joe, if it weren&rsquo;t for politics, I believe we&rsquo;d be good friends.&rdquo; Then, on Tuesday, The New York Times&rsquo; Tyler Pager reported that Biden&rsquo;s book also includes the contents of a letter Trump left for Biden in 2021, when Biden took office. The letter was dated Jan. 20, 2021. That&rsquo;s the day Biden was inaugurated and just a couple of weeks after the Jan. 6 insurrection at the Capitol. Pager wrote, &ldquo;The letter, written on White House stationery with Mr. Trump&rsquo;s signature black Sharpie, is a remarkable document for how discordant it is with Mr. Trump&rsquo;s public comments about Mr. Biden and the 2020 presidential election. Mr. Trump did not criticize Mr. Biden or challenge the results of the election. Instead, he wrote almost poetically about his experience working in the White House and how he believed that Mr. Biden &lsquo;will be true to yourself and the American people.&rsquo;&rdquo; Trump wrote in the letter, &ldquo;Many presidents have come and gone who simply governed as Republicans or Democrats. Largely, they did not change history, they were just swept along in its relentless tide. This will not happen to you because I know how much you love the American people.&rdquo; Biden writes in the book that it's as if &ldquo;there were two Donald Trumps.&rdquo; These days, Trump and his administration are not so kind to Biden, whom they blame for the various troubles America is facing these days. Steven Cheung, the White House communications director, said in a statement Tuesday, &ldquo;Joe Biden should have heeded the president&rsquo;s wise words, but instead he tried to destroy this country and weaponize the justice system against his political enemies. Biden is a disgrace, and America is better off with him no longer as president.&rdquo; VOA director was fired illegally An appeals court ruled Tuesday that Michael Abramowitz, the director of Voice of America, was illegally fired by Kari Lake, who was then helping lead the agency that oversees VOA. Lake fired Abramowitz in 2025 as she worked to dismantle VOA under President Donald Trump&rsquo;s direction. Abramowitz sued, and a U.S. district judge agreed with him. The Washington Post&rsquo;s Scott Nover wrote , &ldquo;U.S. District Judge Royce Lamberth, an appointee of President Ronald Reagan who feuded with Lake and at one point threatened to hold her in contempt of court, agreed with Abramowitz that the firing was illegal. Only a Senate-confirmed body called the International Broadcasting Advisory Board, which President Donald Trump dismantled upon taking office for his second term, had the power to fire the VOA director, Lamberth ruled.&rdquo; On Tuesday, more than a year after Lamberth&rsquo;s ruling, a three-judge appeals court panel unanimously upheld his decision. In a statement, Abramowitz said he is &ldquo;gratified&rdquo; and eager to get back to work. He added, &ldquo;My lawyers and I are carefully studying the decision to determine next steps. But there is no longer any reason for the government to prevent me from doing the job for which I was legally installed on a bipartisan basis more than two years ago.&rdquo; Romo&rsquo;s brief return Tony Romo was back on the air Monday night. Well, at least for a little while. Romo, who parted ways with CBS Sports after being arrested for operating a vehicle while intoxicated over the summer, turned up on Pat McAfee&rsquo;s alternate &ldquo;Monday Night Football&rdquo; broadcast on ESPN2. Romo joined the broadcast for about five minutes of game time during the Buffalo Bills-Minnesota Vikings matchup. He told McAfee, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m doing good.&rdquo; But the conversation stuck to football analysis and didn&rsquo;t really get into any off-the-field stuff. Before Romo left the broadcast, McAfee told him, &ldquo;We missed the hell out of you this season. You need to know that from all of us. We appreciate you, brother.&rdquo; When CBS decided to part ways with Romo, he had $72 million left on his deal. Details of a settlement are unknown, but The Athletic&rsquo;s Andrew Marchand and Mark Puleo reported in early October that Romo &ldquo;will likely be paid a good portion of the remaining money he was owed.&rdquo; The question now is if and when Romo might return to the airwaves on a full-time basis, once again calling games. I remain a big fan of Romo&rsquo;s work, and any network would be smart to bring him back at some point. Media tidbits The Washington Sun&rsquo;s Jenna Monnin with &ldquo;Appeals Court Blocks DOJ&rsquo;s Demand for Journalists&rsquo; LinkedIn Data.&rdquo; Politico&rsquo;s Josh Gerstein with &ldquo;Judge rejects BBC&rsquo;s motion to dismiss Trump libel suit.&rdquo; Semafor&rsquo;s Max Tani with &ldquo;&lsquo;Put the posters in charge&rsquo;: Inside the Democrats&rsquo; secret strategy to flood the internet with clips.&rdquo; The New York Times&rsquo; Valerie Hopkins with &ldquo;The Lights Go Out at Novaya Gazeta, Russia&rsquo;s Bravest Newspaper.&rdquo; And here&rsquo;s The Kyiv Independent&rsquo;s Katie Marie Davies with &ldquo;The quiet death of Novaya Gazeta: How a once-powerful newspaper refused to leave Russia &mdash; and slowly lost its voice.&rdquo; Mediaite&rsquo;s Ana Marie Cox with &ldquo;Sorry, Fox News: The Democrats Are Hot Now.&rdquo; For Poynter&rsquo;s Ask the Recruiter column, Boston Globe managing editor Cristina Silva with &ldquo;Want a newsroom job that doesn&rsquo;t exist? Here&rsquo;s how to pitch it.&rdquo; Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Matt Yoder with &ldquo;Dianna Russini wants us to pretend none of this ever happened.&rdquo; Hot type In a guest essay for The New York Times, writer and former supermodel Paulina Porizkova with &ldquo;The Value I Lost When I Became an Older Woman.&rdquo; The Ringer&rsquo;s Katie Baker with &ldquo;I Spy With My Little AI: A Guide to the Jensen Huang&ndash;Nvidia Scandal.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Transform your editing with our five-week course designed for new editors: Edit to Elevate: Essentials for New Editors . From Jan. 13 to Feb. 10, 2027. Enroll by Jan. 8, 2027 or before seats fill. Build a strong foundation for managing people in our five-day Essential Skills for New Managers , March 1&ndash;5, 2027, in St. Petersburg, Florida. Designed for managers with up to three years of experience, the program covers delegation, feedback, difficult conversations, managing up and leading former peers. Apply by Dec. 7. Tackle your organization&rsquo;s biggest challenges with Poynter Consulting & Coaching . From leadership development and strategic planning to AI, ethics and newsroom transformation, our experts offer customized solutions to help your team navigate change and build for the future. Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or