      "title": "What happened at Pete Hegseth’s first press conference in months",
      "url": "https://www.semafor.com/article/11/08/2026/put-the-posters-in-charge-inside-the-democrats-secret-strategy-to-flood-the-internet-with-clips",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-11T18:42:47.988Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.semafor.com/article/11/08/2026/put-the-posters-in-charge-inside-the-democrats-secret-strategy-to-flood-the-internet-with-clips",
      "contentType": "text/html",
      "pageTitle": "‘Put the posters in charge’: Inside the Democrats’ secret strategy to flood the internet with clips | Semafor",
      "pageDescription": "The national party decided to take a more combative tone, and to lay off its own brand.",
      "evidenceText": "‘Put the posters in charge’: Inside the Democrats’ secret strategy to flood the internet with clips | Semafor Intelligence for the New World Economy Semafor World Economy Exclusive / ‘Put the posters in charge’: Inside the Democrats’ secret strategy to flood the internet with clips Max Tani Media Editor, Semafor Nov 8, 2026, 9:06pm EST Media Politics Share Carlos Barria/Reuters Post Email Whatsapp Copy link Sign up for Semafor Media: Media’s essential read. Read it now . Email address Sign Up The News By January 2025, Paulina Mangubat knew the Democratic National Committee needed to change its digital communications approach dramatically. The organization had been shattered by a second loss to Donald Trump, in which the party had been strategically outmaneuvered by a new alternative media ecosystem that had helped Republicans circumvent traditional gatekeepers. The DNC didn’t have an online creator strategy beyond one Signal group chat with about 20 left-leaning creators, where it occasionally shared messaging. Its brand had never quite recovered from the 2016 campaign, when it was thought to have tipped the scales against then-presidential candidate Bernie Sanders. Voters saw the national party as overly invested in outreach tactics of the past, as exemplified by a widely-mocked February 2025 post in which its official X account shared a long, hard-to-read list of Democratic accomplishments. That spring, staff met at a weeklong offsite to regroup, and Mangubat, the head of content and creative, opened with a presentation of comments the DNC was getting on social media. “Our team took a lot of the online criticism to heart,” she recalled Friday. The committee decided to make a few key changes. Most visibly, it took a more combative and at times personal tone, an approach mirroring the national mood of Democratic voters. It changed the Republican party logo to one labeled “pedo protectors,” invoking Trump’s connections to disgraced financier Jeffrey Epstein. And after White House senior aide Stephen Miller wrote on X that Texas Democrat James Talarico was the party’s “first transgender Senate candidate,” the DNC’s account replied, “shut up you ugly f*ck.” AD The DNC started posting faster, shortening a previously labored back-and-forth approval process to just 15 minutes between drafting and publishing. DNC senior strategist Tim Hogan told Semafor that the committee reoriented its internal leadership structure, empowering its most online staff to make decisions with less oversight from skittish traditional political operatives. “Put the posters in charge,” Hogan said. But it also decided to take a more behind-the-scenes approach. The DNC’s toxic brand among some audiences was hampering the reach of its posts. In early 2025, the organization relaunched its rapid response online efforts under a new brand, @FactPostNews, an account intentionally designed to look like an independent news aggregator, not a messaging arm of the party. “If you talked to the admins of some accounts, they wouldn’t even realize that they were essentially pushing DNC messaging to their audiences who were not explicitly political,” Mangubat said of some users sharing their content. AD Mangubat noted that the DNC had hired researchers to monitor and dig up negative material on Republicans, largely in the form of video clips from little-noticed interviews on podcasts or public channels. It needed a better way, though, to seed that information to its audiences and voters online. So the committee created a clip library, and began to grow its group of unpaid influencers. The DNC would distribute what it saw as embarrassing or damaging clips of Trump or other administration officials to its one-way Signal group chat, which by October had swelled to more than 700 creators. Instead of sharing talking points or bland DNC messaging, the national party’s communications team decided to simply share clips and let the creators decide whether or how to use them. Once the primaries were over, the party set up group chats with Democratic campaigns’ digital directors to help them share negative and positive video clips. Jon Ossoff’s campaign, for example, quickly shared highly-produced clips from his rallies to the Signal chat and the party’s unpaid creators, who helped the Georgia senator become a viral sensation and elevated his profile significantly. AD “We were not really concerned with being credited or receiving attribution,” Mangubat said. “We were mostly interested in increasing the surface area of our messaging on every single part of the internet.” While much of its focus was on X, TikTok, and Instagram Reels, the national party also began ramping up production of original longform content for YouTube. Mangubat said the DNC sent its video team out into the country to find farmers and others impacted by Trump administration decisions and created mini documentaries for YouTube, which it then chopped up into clips and distributed across other social platforms. Max’s view A decade of politics that began with Michelle Obama’s 2016 mantra, “When they go low, we go high,” has concluded with “shut up you ugly f*ck.” For years, the party had struggled to push back against Trump’s messaging; his bluntness, sensationalism, aggression, and comedic looseness were perfectly suited for the algorithmic-driven online communications environment. Rudeness, when in the spirit of resistance to the party’s opponents, has clearly proven popular with its base. But the DNC’s approach may carry more lasting lessons. Clearly, it worked: The clips it distributed to its creator network frequently went viral. The FactPostNews accounts gained millions of new followers and billions of impressions across platforms, and the organization largely avoided becoming a story itself after fumbling the release of its 2024 autopsy report. The party had success whipping up engagement around posts related to food safety issues, which it noticed were performing well, and tried to tie them to Health and Human Services secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. The DNC’s ability to also distance itself from its work by seeding content to seemingly independent creators and accounts is yet another sign that what we see on social media isn’t as organic as it may appear at first glance. With 2028 around the corner, expect more political actors to find sneakily creative ways of getting their messaging out. AD AD Sign up for Semafor Media Media’s essential read. 1x per week Read it now . Email address Sign up for free Sign in Already subscribed? Sign in and we won’t show you this message again. Take me back to the article",
